usb: dwc2: add a manual, interrupt-masked host<->device role knob

The NixVegas badge (Milk-V Duo S / Sophgo SG2000) wires USB_VBUS_DET (the
dwc2 PHY VBUS sense input) to sense the badge's OWN USB_VBUS_EN output --
a self-referential VBUS. dwc2's OTG state machine therefore sees the VBUS
transitions it itself caused, re-evaluates the connector ID / session, and
oscillates host<->device forever; kworker/u4 (the dwc2 OTG work,
dwc2_conn_id_status_change) pins and soft-locks CPU#0. Bench-proven three
times. The only stable stock configs are the fixed roles dr_mode="host"
and dr_mode="peripheral", neither of which can be changed at runtime
(dr_mode is read once by dwc2_get_dr_mode() at probe), and the usual
runtime mechanism -- usb-role-switch on &usb -> dwc2_drd_init() ->
dwc2_ovr_init() -> dwc2_force_mode() -- hangs this SoC in probe
(usb_add_gadget_udc() after dwc2_hcd_init() never returns; see the
armbian 0041 "put the USB port in host mode" patch for that write-up).

This adds a manual role switch that keeps BOTH controllers initialised
(as dr_mode="otg" does) but never lets the OTG state machine run:

  1. A new opt-in DT property "snps,manual-role-switch" sets
     hsotg->manual_role. When set, dwc2_enable_common_interrupts() keeps
     every OTG-state-machine source OUT of GINTMSK -- OTGINT (the GOTGINT
     aggregate), CONIDSTSCHNG (connector-ID change) and SESSREQINT
     (session request) -- so the connector-ID interrupt that a forced
     mode change raises never schedules dwc2_conn_id_status_change and the
     machine stays inert. dwc2_core_init() re-runs
     dwc2_enable_common_interrupts() on every switch, so the mask has to
     be reasserted there, not once at probe.

  2. A sysfs attribute "dwc2_role" on the platform device
     (/sys/devices/.../4340000.usb/dwc2_role) drives the switch by hand:
     "echo host" / "echo device". It does exactly what the OTG worker's
     two branches do, minus the worker: program a FIXED GOTGCTL
     session-valid override (A-valid for host, B-valid for device, plus
     VBUS-valid and the debounce-filter bypass) so the core ignores the
     oscillating USB_VBUS_DET entirely, dwc2_force_mode() into the fixed
     role, re-mask the OTG IRQs (belt-and-braces after dwc2_core_init),
     then start/stop the host controller or the gadget.

  3. VBUS_EN follows the role. usb_vbus is the gpio fixed regulator on
     portb 5 (USB_VBUS_EN); with vbus-supply=<&usb_vbus> on &usb it is
     dwc2's hsotg->vbus_supply. Host start reaches dwc2_vbus_supply_init()
     (regulator_enable -> VBUS_EN high; the badge sources 5V and is the
     host) through dwc2_hcd_start()->start_work->dwc2_host_start()->
     _dwc2_hcd_start(); the device switch calls dwc2_vbus_supply_exit()
     (regulator_disable -> VBUS_EN low) up front, so the badge stops
     sourcing before it becomes a peripheral and only an external host's
     VBUS is present.

This is deliberately NOT wired to usb-role-switch: the class registration
is the thing that hangs probe on this SoC, and its conn-id override path
is the thing that flaps. The knob is the manual, IRQ-masked equivalent.

Companion DT change (in our own board DTS, both arches):

    &usb {
    -   dr_mode = "peripheral";
    +   dr_mode = "otg";
    +   vbus-supply = <&usb_vbus>;
    +   snps,manual-role-switch;
        status = "okay";
    };

dr_mode="otg" is required, not cosmetic: it is what makes dwc2 initialise
BOTH dwc2_hcd_init() and dwc2_gadget_init() at probe, which the runtime
switch needs present. A fixed dr_mode only builds one side (host -> no
gadget, peripheral -> no usb_hcd), so switching would mean calling
dwc2_hcd_init()/dwc2_gadget_init() from a sysfs write -- far more
invasive. "otg" WITHOUT usb-role-switch is the sweet spot: both
controllers up, no drd.c, and our mask stops the SM the otg path would
otherwise run.

